<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<appendix xml:id="app-codebook-reference">
  <title>Codebook and Controlled Vocabulary Reference</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This appendix identifies the authoritative codebook and vocabulary artifacts,
      explains how they fit together, and records their present status. It deliberately
      does not reproduce the complete contents of a versioned database table or source
      spreadsheet. A manually copied table would become stale as soon as the underlying
      artifact changed.
    </p>

    <remark xml:id="rem-app-codebook-status">
      <title>Status: Canonical Artifact Reference</title>
      <p>
        The current frozen codebook is <c>codebook2-june1</c>, containing identifiers
        <c>CS1</c> through <c>CS10</c>. The exact names and definitions must be read from
        that imported, versioned artifact. The controlled vocabulary remains a separate
        evolving artifact and must not be treated as a second codebook.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-app-codebook-artifacts">
    <title>Authoritative Artifacts</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-app-codebook-artifacts">
      <title>Artifacts used to interpret and classify an episode.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="24%"/>
        <col width="20%"/>
        <col width="56%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell>Artifact</cell>
          <cell>Current status</cell>
          <cell>Authority and purpose</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Shared codebook library</cell>
          <cell>Versioned</cell>
          <cell>
            Stores reusable control-structure identifiers, names, definitions,
            boundaries, and examples. It is the stable library from which a
            question-specific working codebook is selected.
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Frozen pivot-question codebook</cell>
          <cell><c>codebook2-june1</c></cell>
          <cell>
            The ten-code <c>CS1</c>–<c>CS10</c> version used for the existing human
            coding. It is the governing codebook for records whose
            <c>codebook_version</c> has this value.
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Derived pivot-question codebook</cell>
          <cell><c>codebook2-june1-2slot</c> (proposed; import pending)</cell>
          <cell>
            A derived two-slot rewrite of the frozen codebook with harvested surface
            markers, proposed as the label <c>codebook2-june1-2slot</c>. The
            derived-vs-frozen rule: derivations are imported under a fresh label and
            the frozen <c>codebook2-june1</c> remains byte-identical. Its import is an
            owner-run work item (CP1.9); until it lands, no record's
            <c>codebook_version</c> carries the derived label.
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Question-specific working codebook</cell>
          <cell>Question-relative</cell>
          <cell>
            The subset of library entries applicable to one question, together with
            their criterion, judgment, and normative-link bindings. Its construction is
            governed by <xref ref="ch-codebook" text="title"/>.
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Controlled vocabulary</cell>
          <cell>Provisional and versioned</cell>
          <cell>
            Provides canonical proposition labels for normalized extraction and
            matching. The source journal records a seeded seventeen-term draft, but does
            not establish that every draft term has completed formal review.
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Question-level analytic frame</cell>
          <cell>Question-relative</cell>
          <cell>
            Records the problem context, representations, normative reference, question
            goals, and valid-inference set used for correctness and coverage decisions.
          </cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-app-codebook-freeze">
    <title>Current Codebook Freeze</title>

    <p>
      The governing freeze label is <c>codebook2-june1</c>. It registers ten entries,
      <c>CS1</c> through <c>CS10</c>, and replaces the earlier planned freeze label
      <c>codebook2-april24</c>. The June 1 artifact was selected because it matches the
      version used for the existing human coding. Historical records retain their own
      version labels; they are not silently relabeled.
    </p>

    <p>
      The source journal reports that the April 24, May 13, and June 1 artifacts use the
      same ten-code scheme and identical descriptions. The only imported difference in
      June 1 is the name of <c>CS1</c>. This small textual difference does not remove the
      need to record the exact version: validation must compare the tool against the
      definitions that governed the human coding.
    </p>

    <warning xml:id="warn-app-codebook-no-manual-copy">
      <title>Do not reconstruct the codebook from examples</title>
      <p>
        The source journal discusses several codes and includes provisional worked
        authoring examples, but it does not reproduce a verified current row for every
        code. Do not infer missing names, definitions, patterns, or boundaries from
        those examples. Generate the complete reference directly from the frozen
        database or imported source artifact.
      </p>
    </warning>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-app-codebook-entry">
    <title>Required Content of a Codebook Entry</title>

    <p>
      A mature codebook entry should provide enough information for a reviewer to
      distinguish a genuine criterion-to-judgment relationship from a keyword
      coincidence. The authoritative entry should contain at least:
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-app-codebook-entry-fields">
      <title>Recommended content of an authoritative codebook entry.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="27%"/>
        <col width="73%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell>Field</cell>
          <cell>Purpose</cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell><c>code_id</c></cell><cell>Stable opaque identifier, interpreted together with the codebook version.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>name</c></cell><cell>Short human-readable label.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>definition</c></cell><cell>The criterion-to-judgment relationship represented by the code.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Inclusion rule</cell><cell>Evidence that is sufficient to make the code a plausible match.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Exclusion rule</cell><cell>Nearby reasoning that must not receive the code.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Positive examples</cell><cell>Responses or episodes that clearly instantiate the entry.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Near misses</cell><cell>Examples that resemble the entry but differ in a decisive way.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Confusable codes</cell><cell>Other entries that reviewers should compare explicitly.</cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell>Surface markers</cell><cell>Optional search aids. They may retrieve candidates but never determine a final match by themselves.</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      Machine-readable patterns and example identifiers may be stored with the entry or
      in related tables. They are implementation aids, not substitutes for the human
      definition and boundary rules.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-app-codebook-vocabulary">
    <title>Controlled Vocabulary</title>

    <p>
      The controlled vocabulary supplies concise canonical forms for propositions that
      may appear as criteria or judgments. It supports normalization, retrieval, and
      machine-readable matching. It does not determine whether a proposition is true,
      whether an inference is valid, or which code should be assigned.
    </p>

    <p>
      Each vocabulary record should preserve:
      <ul>
        <li>the canonical form;</li>
        <li>a readable description;</li>
        <li>the slot or slots in which it may appear;</li>
        <li>attested surface forms;</li>
        <li>its lifecycle status and version history; and</li>
        <li>notes about contested granularity or question dependence.</li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      A term absent from the vocabulary creates an
      <xref ref="g-oov" text="custom">out-of-vocabulary</xref> question. An episode
      expressed with known vocabulary but unsupported by any current code creates a
      <xref ref="g-no-match" text="custom">no-match</xref> question. These statuses
      diagnose different limitations and must remain separate.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-app-codebook-use">
    <title>How Coders Use This Reference</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          During a blind pass, extract the episode without consulting the codebook and
          leave code fields blank.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          During a codebook-assisted pass, compare the complete episode with the current
          question-specific entries. Match the criterion-to-judgment relationship, not
          isolated words.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Record the strongest candidate in <c>code</c> and other defensible candidates
          in <c>alt_codes</c>. Do not force a unique match when the definitions overlap.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Use OOV, no-match, or unresolved status when the current artifacts do not
          support a defensible assignment.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Evaluate <c>c1</c>, <c>c2</c>, and goal coverage from the question's normative
          reference and valid-inference set, not from the code label.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Record <c>codebook_version</c>, <c>vocabulary_version</c>, and the question-frame
          version with every coding record.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-app-codebook-governance">
    <title>Governance and Known Issues</title>

    <p>
      Proposed terms and codes require review across multiple cases. A used identifier
      is not silently deleted or redefined. Changes are accepted through a new version,
      with deprecations and mappings recorded so historical data remain interpretable.
    </p>

    <p>
      The source journal records one unresolved governance issue: some historical coding
      assigns <c>CS2</c>, defined there as a rightmost-column consistency structure, to
      free-variable and number-of-solutions reasoning outside that definition. This
      appendix does not repair those rows. The mismatch must remain visible for
      adjudication, recoding, or an explicit historical mapping decision.
    </p>

    <p>
      Consult <xref ref="app-protocol-summary" text="title"/> for current matching rules,
      <xref ref="app-coding-sheet" text="title"/> for fields.
    </p>
  </section>
</appendix>
